                <head>189. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>At a meeting of the Special Group on September 19, 1963, William Colby of the
                    Central Intelligence Agency referred to a detailed review of Agency operations
                    in several areas including China and Tibet. According to a memorandum of the
                    meeting by Thomas A. Parrott, Colby stated that the review had shown those
                    activities were “not especially productive.” The memorandum continues:
                    “Therefore, the responsible operators have come to the conclusion that probably
                    a change of approach is indicated. This would restrict targets to those of real
                    significance, and would provide sufficient agent forces to accomplish the
                    objective. Sizeable losses would have to be anticipated.” (Central Intelligence
                    Agency, DCI (McCone) Files, Job 80-B01285A, Box 1, 303 Committee
                    Meetings)</p>
            </div>
